  "statement": [
    {
      "type": "p",
      "md": "At the heart of every exchange sits a matching engine. Resting limit orders wait in the book; incoming market orders sweep the best prices on the opposite side until filled or the book runs dry. Priority is *price-time*: better prices trade first, and among orders at the same price the ones that arrived earlier trade first (FIFO). You will build a small but faithful single-instrument matching engine."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Events"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
        "Event",
        "Meaning"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "L side price qty",
          "Add a new resting limit order: side B or S, price in integer cents, positive qty. It never executes on arrival, even if it crosses the book, it just joins its price level."
        ],
        [
          "M side qty",
          "Market order for qty shares. A market BUY sweeps the ask side from the lowest ask upward; a market SELL sweeps the bid side from the highest bid downward."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Matching rules"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "Price priority: a sweeping market order always trades at the best available price of the opposite side first, then moves to the next price when the level is exhausted.",
        "Time priority: within one price level, resting orders trade in the order they were added (FIFO). A partially filled resting order keeps its place at the front of its queue with its remaining quantity.",
        "Each trade fills min(remaining market qty, resting order qty) shares at the resting order's price.",
        "One trade line is printed per (market order, resting order) match. A resting order nibbled by several market orders therefore appears in several trade lines.",
        "A market order larger than the available opposite-side volume simply fills what is there (partial fill). A market order against an empty opposite side trades nothing."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "md": "Print each trade the moment it happens as T price qty (price in dollars with exactly 2 decimals converted from integer cents, qty = shares filled in that match). After all events have been processed, print the remaining book top: BID x.xx or BID, (best remaining bid), then ASK x.xx or ASK, (best remaining ask)."
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "variant": "warn",
      "title": "Levels vanish when drained",
      "md": "When the last order of a price level is fully consumed the level disappears and the sweep continues at the next price. Forgetting to remove drained levels (or to re-evaluate the best price after every fill) is the classic bug in this problem, the sweep loop must look at the book state after EVERY trade, not once at the start."
    }
  ],
